     - Cons: May introduce bias if not handled correctly.
     - Computational Cost: Low.
  
  B) **Encoding**: One-hot encoding for categorical variables.
     - Assumptions: Categorical variables are nominal.
     - Pros: Preserves information.
     - Cons: Increases dimensionality.
     - Computational Cost: Moderate.
  
  C) **Scaling**: Standardization or Min-Max scaling for numeric features.
     - Assumptions: Features are normally distributed.
     - Pros: Improves model performance.
     - Cons: Sensitive to outliers.
     - Computational Cost: Low.

Suitability Analysis:
  - Data characteristics: Missing values, categorical variables, and numeric features.
  - Assumption satisfaction: Check if assumptions hold for the selected methods.
  - Failure modes: Imputation may fail if missingness is not random.

Selected Method: Imputation followed by encoding and scaling, justified by the need to handle missing values and prepare data for modeling.

Quality validation: Check for missing values and data types after preprocessing.
